<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The IContentSourcePort adapter for content that has been PACKED into the shipped
    artefact: a .pck beside a desktop executable, or the assets of an APK.

    Its sibling, Adapters.Content.LocalFile, reads game-data with System.IO. That works in
    a checkout and in the editor and CANNOT work in an exported build — M7-10x finding (1),
    measured in M7-10: `res://data` globalises to a path that does not exist on disk, and a
    resource inside a .pck is reachable only through the engine's own file access. The
    exported game therefore started with no content at all.

    🔒 WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE, PLAIN-C# PROJECT rather than a class in the engine adapter.
    `23` §7.2a (M7-01c) rules that a class able to reach the engine API implements NO port:
    ports are implemented by plain-C# adapters, engine classes are capabilities the
    composition root names. A GodotContentSource : IContentSourcePort would break that rule,
    and X-06 could not fixture it either — M7-01b measured that a GodotSharp call from the
    unit tier is an uncatchable AccessViolationException that kills the test host.

    So the engine half is reduced to IPackedDocumentReader — two members, no port, no engine
    type in this assembly — and it is implemented in the client's own Composition/ directory,
    which is the only place allowed to name both an adapter and an engine capability
    (Only_composition_roots_reference_adapter_projects). What is left here is plain C# with a
    contract fixture, which is the shape M7-01c called "the Platform.Host shape".

    BCL only — no vendor package.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\SlayIdleRepeat.Application\SlayIdleRepeat.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
